speechtext:
I am told by the deputy that the hon. member for Wentworth, last year, in giving his figures had taken the records of vessels not subsidized by this government at all-not all of them but a good many of them. He quotes as instances of vessels not properly equipped, some of those which were not subsidized by the government at all and therefore the government would be in no way responsible and have no control over them. If a shipper saw fit to ship on a tramp steamer or a regular Trans-Atlantic liner, many of which were not subsidized by us at all we would not be responsible under those circumstances. If that were so the statement of the hon. member for Wentworth would have to be dissected in order to find out whether we were paying public money by way of subsidy to steamers that were not carrying out the contract. If they were getting no public money we would have nothing to do with them.
Mr. SPROUT,E. If my memory is not at fault the question was asked: What steamers are we paying subsidies to and which are operating under 'government contract? We were certainly dealing with 28}
these though there might have been some others included that were not under government contract. The hon. minister was defending what was being done and giving explanations. What we wanted to know was what steamers under government contract were violating their contract and why, if they were violating it, the government did not hold them to it. In the next place we wanted to know why the government allowed their expert to stipulate for a certain temperature as being the proper temperature although the government afterwards came to the conclusion that it was not the proper temperature. There is here disclosed a want of knowledge in connection with this contract that otherwise never would have been admitted by the hon. Minister of Agriculture, because, if there is one thing more than another that has impressed itself upon this House it is that everything is stamped with perfection that emanates from his department.
